{% set local_problems %}
<p>Here's what 69,000 acres of lake does to a house:</p>

<p>The water surface evaporates constantly. On a calm summer morning, fog forms over the lake and settles on everything within a mile of the shoreline. By 9 AM, your fascia boards have absorbed moisture that won't fully dry until mid-afternoon — if it dries at all before the evening humidity starts rising again.</p>

<p>We pulled a gutter system off a house on <a href="/recent-projects/">North Lake Drive</a> last year that the homeowner thought was 15 years old. Turns out it was installed in 2016. Eight years. The seams had corroded through, the aluminum was pitted, and the fascia behind it had soft spots every 8-10 feet. That's what lake humidity does to standard residential materials.</p>

<p>The waterfront lots add another layer of complexity. <a href="/recent-projects/">Buck Island</a>, the <a href="/recent-projects/">Peninsula</a>, properties along <a href="/recent-projects/">Wyeth Drive</a> — these lots slope toward the water. That's the point; you paid for the view. But "sloped toward the lake" means roof runoff wants to follow gravity right past your foundation, your retaining walls, your dock infrastructure. Standard downspout extensions don't solve that problem. You need to think about where water actually goes.</p>

<p>Rental properties have a third problem: nobody's watching. The owner visits occasionally. The renters don't report gutter overflow until water's coming inside. By the time someone notices a problem, it's been developing for months. We see rental properties with fascia damage that started as a small leak and turned into a $2,500 repair because nobody caught it early.</p>
{% endset %}

