{% set local_deep_dive %}
<p>Let me explain what "lake effect" actually means in numbers, because it's not just marketing language.</p>

<p><strong>Humidity differential:</strong> Guntersville averages 75-85% relative humidity year-round. Huntsville, 40 miles away, averages 65-75%. That 10-15 point difference sounds small. It's not. At 85% humidity, wood absorbs moisture continuously. At 70%, it can dry between wet periods. That's the difference between fascia that lasts 25 years and fascia that rots in 12.</p>

<p><strong>Fog frequency:</strong> Guntersville sees fog 80+ mornings per year — that's nearly one in four days where your home is literally wrapped in water vapor. Each fog event deposits moisture on exterior surfaces. Lake homes can go weeks without fully drying out.</p>

<p><strong>TVA water management:</strong> The dam controls lake level, which affects the entire peninsula's water table. When TVA holds the lake high for summer recreation, groundwater rises throughout town. Your foundation sits in wetter soil. Your lot drains slower. When they draw down for flood control, the opposite happens. Your drainage system needs to work across both conditions.</p>

<h4>What This Does to Gutter Systems</h4>

<p><strong>Seams fail faster.</strong> Sectional gutter seams rely on sealant to stay watertight. That sealant expands and contracts with temperature, flexes when gutters move, and slowly breaks down from UV exposure. In normal humidity, it lasts 8-12 years. Near the lake, we see failures at 4-6 years. The constant moisture cycle accelerates every degradation process.</p>

<p><strong>Fascia rots from behind.</strong> Here's the insidious part: fascia can look fine from the ground while rotting from the back. Moisture wicks through the wood from the humid air, creating soft spots behind the gutter where you can't see them. By the time paint starts peeling or gutters start pulling away, the damage has spread 6-12 inches in each direction.</p>

<p><strong>Debris becomes sludge.</strong> Leaves and pine needles in a dry gutter eventually blow out or come out easily when cleaned. In a humid gutter, that debris absorbs moisture, compacts, and turns into a dense organic layer that holds water against the metal. We've cleaned gutters on <a href="/recent-projects/">Point of Pines</a> where the debris had basically become soil — things growing in it.</p>

<p><strong>Hangers corrode.</strong> Standard steel hangers rust. The rust spreads to the gutter metal. The hanger weakens. Eventually gutters sag or pull away. On lake homes, we use coated or stainless fasteners. Costs more. Doesn't fail in five years.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>If you own property in Guntersville — whether you live here, vacation here, or rent it out — here's the math that actually matters for gutters.</p>

<h4>Sizing for Lake Conditions</h4>

<p>Standard residential gutters are 5-inch K-style with 2x3 downspouts. That's fine for a ranch house in Madison. It's marginal for most lake homes.</p>

<p>The numbers: a 5-inch gutter holds about 1.2 gallons per linear foot. A 6-inch holds 2.0 gallons — 67% more capacity. Lake homes often have steeper roof pitches (for views), which means water comes off faster. You need the capacity to catch it.</p>

<p>Downspouts matter more than most people realize. A 2x3 downspout handles about 600 square feet of roof area. A 3x4 handles 1,200 — double the capacity. Most lake homes have 2,000+ square feet of roof. Do the math on how many downspouts you actually need versus how many you have.</p>

<p>Our default for Guntersville waterfront: 6-inch gutters with 3x4 downspouts. Costs maybe $300-500 more than standard sizing on a typical house. Handles real storms without backing up.</p>

<h4>Material Selection</h4>

<p>We use .032 gauge aluminum on lake properties instead of the standard .027. That's 18% thicker metal. More rigid, more resistant to denting, more corrosion resistance over time. The cost difference is about $1-2 per linear foot — maybe $200-400 on a whole house. Worth it for the longevity.</p>

<p>Fascia material matters too. If you're replacing rotted fascia, consider composite or PVC instead of wood. Costs about 30% more than primed pine. Won't absorb moisture. Won't rot. Ever. On a lake house where you're already paying for the labor to replace fascia, the material upgrade often makes sense.</p>

<h4>Where the Water Goes</h4>

<p>On a waterfront lot, downspout placement isn't just about getting water away from the foundation. You need to think about:</p>

<p><strong>Slope direction.</strong> Most lake lots slope toward the water. An extension that dumps water 6 feet from the house still puts that water on a path toward your lower level. Underground drains that route around the house are often the real solution.</p>

<p><strong>Retaining walls and seawalls.</strong> Water concentrated behind a retaining wall undermines it. Water flowing over a seawall erodes the cap. Your drainage needs to account for these structures.</p>

<p><strong>Dock and boathouse impact.</strong> Water running down the slope can erode the bank above your dock. If you have a covered dock or boathouse, those structures need drainage too.</p>

<p><strong>Ice in winter.</strong> Water that pools on paths, stairs, or dock approaches becomes a liability when it freezes. Route discharge away from high-traffic areas.</p>

<h4>The Rental Property Calculation</h4>

<p>For rental owners, the math is simple: downtime costs money.</p>

<p>A gutter failure during rental season means emergency calls, unhappy guests, potential refunds. A $2,000 premium for quality installation with guards and oversized systems pays for itself the first time it prevents a problem.</p>

<p>We work with rental owners who've learned this the hard way. The ones who've had water damage during a busy weekend. The ones who've gotten 2 AM calls about overflowing gutters. They all end up at the same conclusion: spend more upfront, sleep better later.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set local_spotlight %}
<p>Guntersville isn't uniform — the north shore, south shore, and downtown each have different characters and different drainage challenges.</p>

<h4>North Shore: <a href="/recent-projects/">North Lake Drive</a>, <a href="/recent-projects/">Brown's Creek</a>, <a href="/recent-projects/">Point of Pines</a></h4>

<p>The north shore faces the open lake. More wind exposure, which helps with drying but also means more storm damage risk. These properties often have the steepest lots — great views, challenging drainage.</p>

<p>We see more erosion problems on the north shore. Water coming off roofs runs downhill fast. If it's not properly channeled, it cuts paths through landscaping and undermines slopes. Underground drainage is common here — the only way to get water past the house without erosion.</p>

<p>The north shore also gets more debris. Wind pushes leaves and pine needles off the trees and into gutters. Properties up here often need quarterly cleaning instead of twice yearly.</p>

<h4>South Shore: <a href="/recent-projects/">South Lake Drive</a>, <a href="/recent-projects/">Wyeth Drive</a>, <a href="/recent-projects/">Buck Island</a></h4>

<p>The south shore is more protected — gentler slopes, less wind exposure. The properties here tend to be a bit older, which means aging gutter systems and more deferred maintenance.</p>

<p>We see more fascia rot on the south shore, probably because the protected location means less wind to dry things out. The humidity just sits there. Houses on Wyeth Drive that looked fine from the street have revealed serious fascia damage when we got up to measure.</p>

<p>The Buck Island area is interesting — technically an island connected by a causeway. Maximum humidity exposure. Properties there need the premium treatment: heavier materials, careful drainage design, diligent maintenance.</p>

<h4>Downtown and Town Center</h4>

<p><a href="/recent-projects/">Downtown Guntersville</a> has character — historic buildings around <a href="/recent-projects/">Gunter Avenue</a>, older homes in the established neighborhoods. These properties face the same humidity as the waterfront, but often with added complications: original gutters from mid-century construction, unique rooflines, decades of accumulated fixes.</p>

<p>We've worked on downtown buildings with three generations of gutter modifications layered on top of each other. The current problem is often the result of previous "solutions" that created new issues. Sometimes the fix is simpler than what's currently installed — remove the complexity, design fresh, install properly.</p>

<h4>Tournament Season Reality</h4>

<p>Guntersville hosts major bass tournaments throughout the year. Tournament weekends pack the town — marinas busy, restaurants full, rental properties booked solid. If you're scheduling gutter work on a rental property, check the tournament calendar. You don't want a work crew showing up when you've got paying guests.</p>

<p>The flip side: tournament weekends are a good time to work on primary residences. We're already in the area. Traffic patterns are predictable. And we know to stay away from the boat ramps.</p>
{% endset %}
